      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Liver abscess is a common deep seated abscess in children; amebic liver abscess is associated with more local complications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE CHARACTERISTICS",
          "content": "We report two preschool children presenting with short history of pain, fever and right upper quadrant pain. The abscess communicated with gastro-intestinal tract (ascending colon in case 1 and duodenum in case 2), and diagnosis of amebic liver abscess was confirmed by DNA PCR."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME",
          "content": "Both children were successfully managed with intravenous antibiotics and catheter drainage."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "MESSAGE",
          "content": "Gastrointestinal fistulization may be rarely seen in amebic liver abscess. Conservative management with antibiotics, catheter drainage and supportive care may suffice."
        }
      ]},
